## The base_sdk / marketplace_sdk split (replaces core_sdk)

`core_sdk` mixed truly universal foundation code with Foodyman-marketplace-specific customer-UI
code that should have had its own SDK all along — `merchants_sdk` already owns the manager/seller
persona, `delivery_sdk` already owns the driver persona, but the customer persona's
marketplace-specific code (search, wallet, become_seller, etc.) never got its own equivalent SDK.

- **`base_sdk`** (new): the true foundation. `AppDatabase`, theme/`AppStyle`, `TokenInterceptor`/
  `HttpService`, routing scaffold, the `UserData`/session model, generic UI primitives (buttons,
  modals, text fields — anything not tied to marketplace concepts), `LocalStorage`/`AppHelpers`/
  `TrKeys`/`DateService` (the persona-neutral versions already identified as the real generic API
  consumed by agent/replay/subscriptions/fav — do not move these to `merchants_sdk`, that would
  recreate coupling already removed this session). Everyone depends on `base_sdk`: `merchants_sdk`,
  `delivery_sdk`, `marketplace_sdk`, `agent_sdk`, `replay_sdk`, and later `lms_sdk`.
- **`marketplace_sdk`** (new): customer-persona marketplace UI — search, wallet, become_seller,
  like/favorites integration, customer home/profile pages. Same tier as `merchants_sdk`/
  `delivery_sdk`. Depends only on `base_sdk`.

A prior audit (before this refork was decided) already produced a detailed file-by-file
categorization proposal for the old `core_sdk`'s contents into base/marketplace/misplaced-persona
buckets, including a specific breakdown of `dependency_manager.dart`'s 191 errors (mostly phantom
imports) and a recommendation to stop centralizing DI there — each persona SDK should register its
own repos via the installer's existing `<Name>SdkDependencies.register()` auto-injection, leaving
a thin base-level bootstrap. Use that reasoning as a starting point, but source real code from
`paas_customer` now that the phantom-vs-real picture is fully known, not from the old `core_sdk`
(most of which doesn't actually exist).

## Local storage / offline-cache decisions already made — apply these when rebuilding LocalStorage-equivalent code

`paas_customer/lib/infrastructure/services/local_storage.dart` was audited. Findings to carry
into the rebuild:

- **Fine to keep cached as-is**: token, `uiType`, own user profile, search history, saved/favorite
  shop IDs, selected address, language/locale settings (including `langLtr`), selected currency,
  translations, theme mode (dark/light), settings list/fetched flag.
- **Real bug to fix, not carry forward**: `walletData` (`Wallet`) is cached as the apparent primary
  read path for a **financial balance**. This is exactly the anti-pattern the offline-cache
  principle (retention/read-order — see `agent/SDK_README.md`'s Offline Persistence section, ADR-004
  in `occultation/.rokct/decision_log.md`) warns against: a stale wallet balance could make a
  customer think a topup succeeded when it didn't, or that they have more money than they do.
  Wallet balance must always be fetched live; local storage at most as a "last known, may be
  stale" fallback display, never the primary source, in whatever SDK ends up owning wallet
  concerns (`wallet_sdk` or `marketplace_sdk`, your call based on what's actually there).
- **Check, don't assume**: `getDeliveryInfo()` reads a key literally named `carInfo` but returns a
  `DeliveryResponse` — driver-app vocabulary sitting inside the customer app's LocalStorage.
  Determine whether this is dead code (likely residue from the `paas_customer`+`paas_driver`+
  `paas_manager` merge attempt that produced `RokctApp/lib_backup` — do not reference or touch that
  folder, it's an inert backup) or something real, and place it correctly (`delivery_sdk`?
  genuinely unused?) rather than carrying the confusing naming forward.

## templates/ pattern — mostly retired, understand why before you touch any manifest.json

The `templates/` copy-out-to-host-app-`lib/` pattern existed because of an assumption that turned
out not to hold in this system: it was built assuming Flutter's own pub-cache (fetched via
`flutter pub`, immutable, hidden from the developer) would hold SDK code, so anything a developer
needed to edit had to be physically copied out into the host app's own `lib/` at install time.
That's not how this system actually works — `sdk_composer.py`'s git-based compose flow caches
everything in a fully-editable local `.rokct/cache/<name>/`, so there's no immutability problem to
work around.

New rule for this rebuild:
- **Default: code stays inside the owning SDK's own `lib/`**, imported normally via
  `package:<sdk_name>/...`. This includes theme — `base_sdk` should own its theme files directly,
  not install them out to `lib/core/presentation/theme/` in every host app.
- **Exception — `main.dart`**: Flutter requires exactly one at the app root; it can't live inside
  a package. This gets injected by whichever SDK is `home_sdk` for a given composed app (matching
  the existing installer mechanism), not copied via `templates/`.
- **Exception — the home/main page**: likely needs the same `home_sdk`-injection treatment as
  `main.dart`, for the same reason (inherently per-app/product-specific at the top level, even
  though it's not a native platform file).
- **Exception — native platform files**: `android/`, `ios/`, `res/`, app icons, and similar
  platform-project-structure files stay in `templates/` since a Dart package genuinely cannot
  contain them.

Update each rebuilt SDK's `manifest.json` accordingly — most `installs`/`routes` entries that
exist purely to copy Dart source out to the host app should disappear; only the `main.dart`/
home-page injection and native platform file installs should remain as `templates/`-based.

## What to report back before proceeding to Supacharge

1. Confirmation `paas_customer` itself still builds and runs correctly once its code has been
   moved out into the new SDK set (it should be consuming the SDKs it now uses, not its own
   duplicated copies).
2. The final `base_sdk`/`marketplace_sdk` file disposition actually used (the prior audit's
   categorization was a proposal — real `paas_customer` source may differ from what the phantom
   `core_sdk` imports implied).
3. Resolution of the `carInfo`/`DeliveryResponse` and `walletData` items above.
4. Any place `paas_customer`'s real code revealed something materially different from what the
   phantom `core_sdk` imports assumed (this is expected and worth surfacing, not a problem to
   hide).

Do not start on Supacharge until this phase is confirmed working and reported.
